isomd5sum provides a way of making use of the ISO9660 application data
area to store md5sum data about the iso.  This allows you to check the
iso given nothing more than the iso itself.

In addition to the md5sum, the serial number of an X.509 certificate can
optionally be embedded into the same application data area.  This is useful
for recording which certificate an image is associated with.  The serial is
stored exactly as reported by

  openssl x509 -in cert.pem -noout -serial
  serial=21B3CA436F1D60287FDA31DA91F5F697

To implant both the md5sum and a certificate serial number into an iso::

  implantisomd5 --cert /etc/pki/tls/certs/cert.pem image.iso

The certificate may be in PEM or DER format.  The embedded serial can be
read back together with the md5sum information::

  checkisomd5 --md5sumonly image.iso

Embedding a certificate serial does not affect md5 verification: the whole
application data area is excluded from the checksum, so "checkisomd5" still
passes.  Building isomd5sum requires OpenSSL (libcrypto) for this feature.

Cryptographic signing
----------------------
The embedded md5sum can additionally be signed so that a consumer can prove
the image's checksum was produced by the holder of a certificate's private
key.  The signature is embedded INSIDE the image: its base64 form is written
into the first sector of the trailing skip-sectors region, which is excluded
from the md5 so the signature does not change the checksum it covers.  No
detached "<iso>.sig" file is produced, so the image is self-contained.  RSA,
ECDSA and Ed25519 keys are supported.

To sign while implanting::

  implantisomd5 --cert cert.pem --sign-key key.pem image.iso
  # writes only image.iso (the signature is stored inside it)

To verify the signature against the certificate's public key::

  checkisomd5 --cert cert.pem image.iso

"checkisomd5" first performs the normal md5 media check and then reports the
signature result as "Signature status: VALID" or "INVALID".  A failed or
missing signature yields a non-zero exit status.  Note that the md5 check and
the signature check are complementary: the md5 check detects tampering with
the image content, while the signature proves the embedded md5 was authorized
by the certificate holder.

Distributing a signed image
---------------------------
Verification requires only TWO things, provided together:

  1. the image            e.g.  rear.iso   (the signature is embedded in it)
  2. the certificate      e.g.  cert.pem   (public; safe to distribute)

Because the signature lives inside the image there is no separate file that
can be lost or renamed.  If an image carries no embedded signature,
"checkisomd5 --cert" reports "Signature status: MISSING".  The private key
used for signing stays on the signing host and is NEVER needed (or shipped)
for verification.

Validating the certificate's trust chain
----------------------------------------
By default "checkisomd5 --cert" trusts the public key inside the given
certificate; it does not validate the certificate's trust chain, expiry or
revocation.  Pass "--ca <ca-bundle.pem>" and/or "--CApath <cadir>" to have the
certificate validated against one or more trusted CA certificates (via
OpenSSL's X509_verify_cert) BEFORE its key is trusted::

  checkisomd5 --cert cert.pem --ca ca-bundle.pem rear.iso
  checkisomd5 --cert cert.pem --CApath /etc/pki/tls/certs rear.iso

"--ca" takes a PEM bundle file (equivalent to "openssl verify -CAfile") while
"--CApath" takes a directory of CA certificates hashed with c_rehash
(equivalent to "openssl verify -CApath").  Either or both may be given; with
both, the certificate is trusted if it chains to a CA in either location.

The certificate must chain to one of those CAs and be within its validity
dates.  If it does not, the result is reported as "Signature status:
UNTRUSTED" and the signature is rejected even if the key would otherwise
verify it, yielding a non-zero exit status.  "--ca"/"--CApath" require
"--cert"; revocation (CRL/OCSP) is still not checked.  Without them, chain
validation is skipped and only the key is used (the previous behaviour), which
remains equivalent to validating the certificate separately with e.g.
"openssl verify -CAfile ca-bundle.pem cert.pem".

Packaging and distribution requirements
---------------------------------------
The signing/certificate features add an OpenSSL dependency on top of the
historical popt dependency.  When building a package or distributing the
binaries, the following are required.

Build dependencies (on the build host)::

  RPM (isomd5sum.spec):   gcc  make  popt-devel     openssl-devel  python3-devel
  DEB (debian/control):   gcc  make  libpopt-dev    libssl-dev     python3-dev
                          (+ debhelper-compat (= 13), dh-python)

openssl-devel / libssl-dev is the key addition: without it signature.c and
certificate.c will not compile.

Runtime dependencies (on the target machine)::

  implantisomd5 / checkisomd5 are dynamically linked against:
    libcrypto (OpenSSL)   - the --cert / --sign-key signing logic
    libpopt               - command-line option parsing

  RHEL / Fedora:   openssl-libs   popt
  Debian / Ubuntu: libssl3        libpopt0   (libssl1.1 on older releases)

These are resolved automatically when the package is installed: rpm derives
them from the ELF binaries, and the deb declares "Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}".

What to ship for a signed image like::

  implantisomd5 --cert /etc/pki/tls/certs/cert.pem \
                --sign-key /etc/pki/tls/private/cert.key \
                --force /var/lib/rear/output/rear.iso

  * Build and distribute the rear-isomd5sum RPM ("make rpm") or .deb
    ("make deb"), NOT the raw binary, so the OpenSSL/popt runtime deps are
    declared and resolved.  This package Provides/Obsoletes the stock
    isomd5sum and cleanly supersedes it.
  * The X.509 certificate (PEM or DER) is public and may ship with the image;
    its serial number is embedded into the ISO.
  * The private key (--sign-key) is a site secret: keep it on the signing
    host only (e.g. /etc/pki/tls/private/, mode 0600, root-only).  It is
    NEVER needed for verification and must not be packaged.
  * On the verifying side only checkisomd5 + openssl-libs are needed; the
    certificate must travel with the image (the signature is already embedded
    inside the image - see "Distributing a signed image" above).


This fork of isomd5sum is hosted on GitHub. The source can be
grabbed via git with
  git clone https://github.com/gdha/isomd5sum.git
or browsed via
  https://github.com/gdha/isomd5sum

The original isomd5sum is hosted on GitHub. The source can be
grabbed via git with
  git clone https://github.com/rhinstaller/isomd5sum.git
or browsed via
  https://github.com/rhinstaller/isomd5sum

Releases of isomd5sum are automatically generated by the tags. eg. for the
1.0.12 release::
  https://github.com/rhinstaller/isomd5sum/archive/1.0.12.tar.gz

You can send questions, enhancements, etc to anaconda-devel-list@redhat.com.
